import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-chat-input',
  template: `
    <input [value]="draft()" (input)="onInput($event)"
           (keyup.enter)="send()" (keydown.escape)="draft.set('')" />
    <button (click)="send()" [disabled]="!draft()">Send</button>
    @for (m of messages(); track $index) { <p>{{ m }}</p> }
  `,
})
export class ChatInput {
  protected draft = signal('');
  protected messages = signal<string[]>([]);

  protected onInput(event: Event) {
    this.draft.set((event.target as HTMLInputElement).value);
  }
  protected send() {
    if (!this.draft().trim()) return;
    this.messages.update((list) => [...list, this.draft()]);
    this.draft.set('');
  }
}

concepts.body:
Templates have built-in **control flow blocks**, written with `@`. (Older code uses `*ngIf` and `*ngFor` attributes for the same jobs.)

**`@if`**, with optional `@else if` and `@else`, shows content only when a condition is true:

```html
@if (user(); as u) { <p>Hi {{ u.name }}</p> } @else { <a>Log in</a> }
```

The `as` part stores the condition's value under a name, handy when it comes from a signal or might be `null` or `undefined`.

**`@for`** repeats a block for each item, and a **`track` expression is required**:

```html
@for (item of items(); track item.id; let i = $index, last = $last) { ... } @empty { <p>No items</p> }
```

`track` tells Angular how to recognise each item, so when the array changes it can move, add and remove just the affected elements instead of rebuilding the whole list. Use a unique id; `track $index` is fine only for lists that never change. Built-in variables include `$index`, `$count`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even` and `$odd`. The `@empty` block shows when the list is empty.

**`@switch`** picks one case using strict equality (`===`):

```html
@switch (status()) { @case ('loading') { <spinner /> } @case ('error') { <p>Failed</p> } @default { <app-list /> } }
```

Blocks need no imports and work with TypeScript narrowing: inside `@if (result.kind === 'ok')`, Angular's type checking knows the narrowed type. For complicated conditions, use a `computed` signal so the template stays readable.

