concepts.body:
**Signal forms** (stable in Angular 22, from `@angular/forms/signals`) build a form on top of a **writable signal holding your data**, for example the email and password of a login form. The second argument of `form()` is a **schema**, a function that lists the rules each field must follow; checking input against such rules is called **validation**:

```ts
model = signal({ email: '', password: '' });
loginForm = form(this.model, (p) => {
  required(p.email, { message: 'Email is required' });
  email(p.email);
  minLength(p.password, 8);
});
```

`form()` returns a **field tree** with the same shape as the data: `loginForm.email`, `loginForm.password`, and deeper fields for nested objects and arrays. What the user types is written straight into the model signal, so `model()` is always the current form value.

Connect inputs with the **`[formField]`** **directive** (a helper that adds behaviour to an element; import `FormField`): `<input [formField]="loginForm.email" />`. It keeps the value, disabled and touched state of native inputs in sync, and custom controls can opt in.

Each field is callable and returns its **state** as signals: `loginForm.email().value()`, `valid()`, `invalid()`, `errors()`, `touched()` (the user has visited it), `dirty()` (the user has changed it), `pending()`. The root `loginForm()` combines the state of all fields.

The schema function also supports `pattern`, `min`/`max`, custom `validate(path, fn)`, `validateHttp` for checks that ask the server, conditional `required(path, { when })`, `disabled(path, fn)` and `hidden(...)`.

For submission, `submit(form, action)` marks all fields touched, runs the async action only if the form is valid, tracks `submitting()`, and puts the errors the server sends back onto the matching fields. Reactive forms (`FormGroup`) remain fully supported for existing code.

concepts.code:
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { form, FormField, required, email, minLength, submit } from '@angular/forms/signals';
@Component({
  selector: 'app-login', imports: [FormField],
  template: `
    <form (submit)="onSubmit($event)">
      <input type="email" [formField]="loginForm.email" />
      @if (loginForm.email().touched() && loginForm.email().invalid()) {
        @for (e of loginForm.email().errors(); track e.kind) { <small>{{ e.message ?? e.kind }}</small> }
      }
      <input type="password" [formField]="loginForm.password" /> <button>Log in</button>
    </form>`,
})
export class Login {
  protected model = signal({ email: '', password: '' });
  protected loginForm = form(this.model, (p) => {
    required(p.email, { message: 'Email is required' });
    email(p.email, { message: 'Not a valid email' });
    minLength(p.password, 8);
  });
  protected async onSubmit(event: Event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    await submit(this.loginForm, async () => { console.log('sending', this.model()); return undefined; });
  }
}

import { Component, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { toObservable, toSignal, takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { debounceTime, distinctUntilChanged, switchMap, interval } from 'rxjs';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-search', template: `
    <input (input)="query.set($any($event.target).value)" placeholder="Search users" />
    @for (u of results(); track u.id) { <p>{{ u.name }}</p> }`,
})
export class Search {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  protected query = signal('');
  protected results = toSignal(
    toObservable(this.query).pipe(
      debounceTime(300),                       // wait for typing to pause
      distinctUntilChanged(),
      switchMap((q) => this.http.get<{ id: number; name: string }[]>(`/api/users?q=${q}`)),
    ),                                         // switchMap cancels stale requests
    { initialValue: [] },
  );
  constructor() {
    interval(60_000).pipe(takeUntilDestroyed()).subscribe(() => console.log('tick')); // auto-unsubscribes
  }
}

